# E36 -- Is FREQUENCY a comparative weight or an independent probability? The wiki calls it 'a comparative number', and E34 hinted at it — when seven suppressed species were restored, the OTHER cavern species fell 39 to 0 and 27 to 12 while an untouched control's held steady, which looks like crowding. The mix cannot separate the models: a ratio of independent probabilities tracks the frequency ratio too. So both arms hold the SAME 4:1 ratio at very different absolute levels — TROGLODYTE and ELK_BIRD at 100 and 25 against 4 and 1 — with every other species left at its natural frequency. NOTHING is zeroed: E35's first attempt isolated the pair by zeroing 81 species and hit the CREEPY_CRAWLER freeze (addendum 54). A season, two replicates each, tool disabled, cavern counts excluding the magma sea and underworld.
run 20260918-151207 fort CTRL backup CTRL.preverify
DF v0.53.16 win64 STEAM DFHack 53.16-r1.1 world The Legendary Dimension site 32 tiles 29..29,20..20 seed WILDERPOP1
commits: DwarfCron b2cc7b1 seasonal-wildlife e7a3d80 fps cap 1000

## Predictions (written before the run)
- comparative — a shared spawn budget: the two species' SHARE of cavern arrivals rises sharply in the high arm at the expense of everything else, while total cavern arrivals stay about the same in both arms
- independent probability per species: the two species' own counts scale with their frequencies (roughly 25-fold between arms) while the other species' counts are unaffected, so the total rises in the high arm
- neither cleanly: the two species' counts do not track their frequencies; frequency is not a proportional lever at all

